<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cihuacōātl</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cihuacoatl, Quilaztli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cihuacōātl, sometimes known as Quilaztli, was one of a number of motherhood and fertility goddesses in Aztec mythology, associated especially with midwives and the sweat lodges where they worked, and patroness of the city of Culhuacan. In tradition she helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl">Quetzalcoatl</a> create the present humanity by grinding the bones of earlier ages and mixing them with his blood; she was usually shown as a fierce skull-faced old woman bearing a warrior's spears and shield. Her name was also the title of a high official of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, second to the tlatoani, who supervised internal affairs; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacaelel">Tlacaelel</a> held it under four emperors in the 15th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cihuacoatl.jpg" alt="Cihuacōātl" width="330" height="576" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Madman2001 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cihuacoatl_statue_(Museo_Nacional_Antropologia).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Beneath him stood the <b>cihuacoatl</b>, a chief minister who managed internal affairs; tribute stewards (calpixque) who collected and recorded tribute in the provinces; and the senior commanders, the tlacatecatl and tlacochcalcatl.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>He commanded the army, decided war and diplomacy, received the tribute of the provinces and was the final judge, while a close kinsman, the <b>cihuacoatl</b>, governed the city itself.</blockquote><a href="/societies/aztec-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Aztec Empire</a></li></ul></section>
